Guided Exercise: Create Linux Containers and Kubernetes Pods
This exercise mainly teaches:
•	Creating pods
•	Checking UID/GID security
•	Viewing logs
•	Attaching to containers
•	Inspecting containers on the node
•	Understanding namespaces
•	Comparing container vs host OS
This is done in Red Hat OpenShift, which runs containers using the CRI-O container runtime and manages containers using Kubernetes concepts.
________________________________________
1. Prepare the Lab Environment
Command:
lab start pods-containers
Purpose:
•	Sets up the lab environment
•	Creates required images, nodes, and cluster configuration
________________________________________
2. Login to OpenShift Cluster
Command:
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
Explanation:
Part	Meaning
oc	OpenShift CLI
login	authenticate to cluster
-u developer	username
-p developer	password
URL	cluster API endpoint
Result → You are now connected to the OpenShift cluster.
________________________________________
3. Create a Project
Command:
oc new-project pods-containers
Explanation:
A project in OpenShift is similar to a namespace in Kubernetes.
Purpose:
•	isolates resources
•	controls security
•	manages users and quotas
Result:
Now using project "pods-containers"
________________________________________
4. Check UID and GID Range
Command:
oc describe project pods-containers
Important output:
openshift.io/sa.scc.uid-range=1000760000/10000
openshift.io/sa.scc.supplemental-groups=1000760000/10000
Meaning:
Value	Meaning
1000760000	starting UID
10000	number of IDs available
So container users can have IDs:
1000760000 → 1000769999
Why?
OpenShift assigns random non-root users to containers for security.
________________________________________
5. Create Pod as Developer
Command:
oc run -it ubi9-user --restart Never \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/ubi9/ubi \
-- /bin/bash -c "whoami && id"
Explanation:
Part	Meaning
oc run	create pod
ubi9-user	pod name
--restart Never	run once
--image	container image
-it	interactive terminal
whoami	show username
id	show UID and GID
Output example:
1000760000
uid=1000760000 gid=0(root) groups=0(root),1000760000
Meaning:
Field	Meaning
UID	1000760000
GID	root group
groups	root + project group
Important:
•	UID = project UID range
•	Container does NOT run as root
•	Container is unprivileged
Security benefit:
•	Container cannot modify host OS.
________________________________________
6. Delete the Pod
Command:
oc delete pod ubi9-user
Purpose:
Remove the pod after testing.
________________________________________
7. Login as Admin
Command:
oc login -u admin -p redhatocp
Admin users have cluster-level privileges.
________________________________________
8. Create Pod Again (Admin)
Command:
oc run -it ubi9-user --restart Never \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/ubi9/ubi \
-- /bin/bash -c "whoami && id"
Output:
root
uid=0(root) gid=0(root)
Difference:
Developer Pod	Admin Pod
UID = project UID	UID = 0
Non-root	Root
Restricted	Privileged
Meaning:
Cluster admins can create privileged containers.
________________________________________
9. Create Pod to Run Date Command
Login again as developer:
oc login -u developer -p developer
Create pod:
oc run ubi9-date --restart Never \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/ubi9/ubi -- date
Explanation:
This pod:
•	starts container
•	runs date
•	exits.
________________________________________
10. View Container Logs
Command:
oc logs ubi9-date
Output example:
Mon Nov 28 15:02:55 UTC 2022
Meaning:
•	logs show stdout of container process
Delete pod:
oc delete pod ubi9-date
________________________________________
11. Create Interactive Pod
Command:
oc run ubi9-command -it \
--image registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/ubi9/ubi -- /bin/bash
Result:
bash-5.1$
You are inside the container shell.
Exit:
exit
________________________________________
12. View Logs
Command:
oc logs ubi9-command
Output:
bash-5.1$
Reason:
No command executed yet.
________________________________________
13. Attach to Running Container
Command:
oc attach ubi9-command -it
Attach to container terminal again.
Run command:
while true; do echo $(date); sleep 2; done
Explanation:
Loop prints date every 2 seconds.
Example output:
Mon Nov 28 15:15:16
Mon Nov 28 15:15:18
________________________________________
14. Check Logs from Another Terminal
Command:
oc logs ubi9-command --tail=10
Meaning:
Option	Purpose
--tail=10	show last 10 log lines
Logs show the date loop output.
________________________________________
15. Get Container Name
Command:
oc get pod ubi9-command -o json | jq .status.containerStatuses[].name
Output:
"ubi9-command"
Meaning:
Pod contains one container with same name.
________________________________________
16. Find Node Hosting the Pod
Command:
oc get pods ubi9-command -o wide
Example output:
NODE: master01
Meaning:
Pod runs on node master01.
________________________________________
17. Debug Node
Developer cannot debug nodes:
oc debug node/master01
Error:
Forbidden
Only admins can debug nodes.
Login as admin and run:
oc debug node/master01
Inside debug pod:
chroot /host
Meaning:
Access host OS filesystem and tools.
________________________________________
18. Find Container ID
Command:
crictl ps --name ubi9-command -o json | jq -r .containers[0].id
Example output:
81adbc6222d79ed9...
Save variable:
CID=$(crictl ps --name ubi9-command -o json | jq -r .containers[0].id)
________________________________________
19. Get Container PID
Command:
crictl inspect $CID | grep pid
Example:
pid: 365297
Save:
PID=365297
________________________________________
20. Check Namespaces
Command:
lsns -p $PID
Output shows namespaces:
Namespace	Purpose
pid	process isolation
net	network isolation
mnt	filesystem isolation
ipc	inter-process communication
uts	hostname isolation
Containers use Linux namespaces for isolation.
________________________________________
21. Compare Host vs Container OS
Host OS:
cat /etc/redhat-release
Example:
Red Hat Enterprise Linux CoreOS 4.18
Container OS:
crictl exec -it $CID cat /etc/redhat-release
Example:
Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9.6
Meaning:
Container runs different OS environment than host.
________________________________________
22. Compare Package Versions
Host:
rpm -qi libgcc
Container:
crictl exec -it $CID rpm -qi libgcc
Versions may differ.
This proves containers have separate software environments.
________________________________________
23. Exit Debug Pod
Commands:
exit
exit
This:
1.	exits host root
2.	removes debug pod
________________________________________
24. Stop Container Loop
Inside pod terminal:
Ctrl + C
exit
Check pod status:
oc get pods
Output:
ubi9-command Running
Pod is still running.
